// ADR 0075 D1 (register D87): a `k(v)` whose resume BUBBLES leaves the arm, so it
// drains the arm's scopes before branching back to the dispatch loop.
//
// `two()` performs twice, so the first resume of every `handle` below bubbles: the
// resumed computation performs again, `k(v)` stores the new kont into the dispatch slot
// and branches to the top of the loop. That branch leaves the arm's block without
// reaching its end-of-block drops, and before this ADR nothing else drained them — so
// every heap binding the arm held was abandoned, once per bubble. The exit code is the
// cheap half and does not move: each arm below answered 42 before the fix too. What
// changed is memory. Measured with `simple`'s shape in a helper fn called from a loop,
// kernel peak working set read after exit: 36.5 MB at 600,000 calls and 147.2 MB at
// 3,000,000 before, 8.8 and 9.0 after — level with the same arm holding an `i64`
// instead. That is about 48 bytes a call: one four-element array, plus allocator
// overhead, for each of the two arm activations that bubbles.
//
// The four shapes pin the floor's reach — every frame at or above the arm's, innermost
// first, and nothing below it:
//
//   - `simple`  — one frame, the arm's block;
//   - `nested`  — a block inside the arm, so two frames are open at the `k(v)`;
//   - (a `while` written INSIDE the arm, around the `k(v)`, would open a body frame at
//     the bubble too. It is not here: ADR 0075 D6 classifies such a `k(v)` (A), because
//     replaying its remainder would re-run the loop from the top, so its bubble aborts
//     and that drain is unreachable. The frames a bubble can actually be holding are the
//     arm's own and any nested block's, which is what `nested` pins.)
//   - `enclosing` — the other end of the floor: `below` belongs to the FUNCTION around
//                 the `handle`, which a bubble does not leave, so it must NOT be drained
//                 here. A floor set any lower frees it on the bubble path and again at
//                 the end of `enclosing`.
//
// The same drop is emitted a second time on the pure path. Unlike `break` / `continue`,
// the two blocks are NOT mutually exclusive — the bubble branches back to the dispatch
// loop, which re-enters the arm and can reach the pure path's drop. What is exclusive is
// the two paths through one ENTRY of the arm: an entry either bubbles and drains here, or
// completes and drops at its block's end, never both. So an entry frees exactly what it
// allocated, which holds only because ADR 0075 D3 refuses an arm binding that takes over
// an allocation made outside the arm (`sentinel::borrow::moved_in_handler_arm`,
// `tests/ui/c75_move_into_handler_arm.sentinel`).
//
// The IR is pinned twice — `d87_a_bubbling_resume_drains_the_arms_scopes` in
// `sentinel-codegen` reads inkwell's, `llvm_a_bubbling_resume_drains_the_arms_scopes` in
// `crates/sentinel-driver/tests/llvm.rs` reads the text oracle's over THIS file — and both
// count the frees on the pure path as well, because an implementation that MOVED the drops
// onto the bubble instead of adding them passes a bubble-only check and leaks at the
// unfixed rate. `scg` is held to the oracle byte-for-byte on this file by the codegen
// differential's corpus sweep.
//
// Exits 42: 42 + 42 + 42 - 84.

effect Io { read() -> i64; }

fn two() -> i64 ! { Io } {
    let a: i64 = perform Io.read();
    let b: i64 = perform Io.read();
    a + b
}

fn simple() -> i64 {
    handle two() with {
        Io.read(k) => { let v: [i64] = [1, 2, 3, 4]; k(v[0] + 20) }
    }
}

fn nested() -> i64 {
    handle two() with {
        Io.read(k) => {
            let outer: [i64] = [5, 6];
            { let inner: [i64] = [7, 8]; k(outer[0] + inner[0] + 9) }
        }
    }
}

fn enclosing() -> i64 {
    let below: [i64] = [9, 9];
    let r: i64 = handle two() with {
        Io.read(k) => { let v: [i64] = [1, 2, 3, 4]; k(v[0] + 20) }
    };
    r + below[0] - 9
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    simple() + nested() + enclosing() - 84
}
